00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:31.020
How are you doing?

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:31.770
BRENDAN BURNS: Hi.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:32.370
Doing great.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So, for those that

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.590
don't know who Brendan Burns
is, he's a famous person that

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:42.530
made a video about what is
faster-- to start a GKE cluster

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:43.820
or to make a latte.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:44.450
BRENDAN BURNS: That's
true, actually.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:45.800
And it's gotten
faster, so I'm going

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.570
to have to up my
latte-making game, I think.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.486
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that is pretty awesome.

00:01:49.486 --> 00:01:51.320
So, tell us a little
bit about yourself.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:51.870
Who are you?

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.330
What do you do at Google?

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:54.163
BRENDAN BURNS: Sure.

00:01:54.163 --> 00:01:56.510
So, I've done a lot
of things at Google.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.260
I've been at Google
almost eight years.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.920
But in the Google
Cloud Platform,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.590
what I've done
recently is I have,

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.160
with a couple of
co-collaborators

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.669
a couple years ago, had this
crazy idea that maybe we

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:11.370
should open source
some of our ideas

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.660
in cluster management, the Borg
software that runs everything

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.200
from search to Gmail
and everything,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.800
and actually all of
the VMs and everything

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.200
that the Google
Cloud Platform runs,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.824
that we should build
out an open source

00:02:20.824 --> 00:02:22.030
version of that software.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.890
And we started doing that.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.410
It came out a little
under two years ago

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:30.930
as a project called Kubernetes
and it's grown from there.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.020
And so, we just keep trying
to push that forward and help

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.400
everybody compute like Google
does, whether they-- actually,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.750
honestly, whether they're on the
Google Cloud Platform or not.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:47.169
So, really believe that we want
it to go into people's data

00:02:47.169 --> 00:02:49.460
centers, as well as being
available on the Google Cloud

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.770
and wherever they happen to
be running their compute.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.186
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, we actually

00:02:53.186 --> 00:02:55.660
had some cool demos
about it today.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:59.125
We interviewed Kelsey
Hightower two weeks ago or so

00:02:59.125 --> 00:03:00.572
about Kubernetes,
one of the two.

00:03:00.572 --> 00:03:02.280
Can you tell us a
little bit about what's

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.529
your favorite feature from
Kubernetes, one of the two,

00:03:04.529 --> 00:03:05.660
if you had to choose one.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.285
BRENDAN BURNS: If I
have to choose one,

00:03:07.285 --> 00:03:09.185
I mean it's like your children.

00:03:09.185 --> 00:03:12.020
You're not supposed to choose
your favorite children.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:12.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: They're
not listening now.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:13.800
BRENDAN BURNS: Oh,
they're not listening?

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:14.800
All right, I don't know.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.954
The features have ears.

00:03:16.954 --> 00:03:18.120
What is my favorite feature?

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.950
Well, I mean, I'm really happy
to see a couple of things

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.510
go GA.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:30.500
So, I'm really happy to see the
work on things like autoscaling

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.070
and stuff like
that get to a place

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:36.710
where it's done-- I mean,
well, nothing's actually done,

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.219
but-- to the point where we
really say this is reliable.

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.260
This is production grade.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.310
You can go out and do it.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.750
I think it's important to
introduce new features.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:45.830
I'm excited to see
things like the Daemon

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:47.130
Set and the ConfigMap.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.542
I'll talk a little
bit more about those.

00:03:49.542 --> 00:03:51.250
It's important to
introduce new features,

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:52.749
but it's also
important to make sure

00:03:52.749 --> 00:03:57.229
that things move through that
beta production ready out there

00:03:57.229 --> 00:03:58.770
and people can use
it, kind of cycle,

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.130
because it shows
that we're not just

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:01.340
throwing new ideas out there.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.770
We're actually finishing
off the ideas that we have.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.904
I'm really excited
to see that happen.

00:04:06.904 --> 00:04:08.070
I'm actually really excited.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.130
ConfigMap is one of those
things I'm really excited about.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.430
Config is a big,
important problem

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.840
for people doing
distributed systems,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.790
and so I'm excited
to see that happen.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.630
And I'm also excited because
it's an example of something

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:24.310
that was largely built by
people outside of Google,

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:26.790
and so that was always
the promise of open source

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.110
was the community, and
community development,

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.450
and engaging with
people who are using it.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.412
And so, it's really nice
to see that as well.

00:04:33.412 --> 00:04:34.870
It's a great feature
for everybody,

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:37.462
but it's a great feature
that Google didn't build.

00:04:37.462 --> 00:04:38.420
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:41.480
So, of course now I need to
ask, what exciting things

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.790
do you think you're going
to see in the future?

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:45.665
BRENDAN BURNS: So, what
I was just working on

00:04:45.665 --> 00:04:49.150
on the plane on the way down,
which is most of the way in,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.690
but didn't quite
make it into 1.2

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:56.310
is the idea of third party
extensions to the API.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:59.150
And so, what this basically
allows you do with your cluster

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.162
is introduce your own API types.

00:05:01.162 --> 00:05:01.870
MARK MANDEL: Ooh.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:02.745
BRENDAN BURNS: Right.

00:05:02.745 --> 00:05:05.252
So without recompiling it,
without doing anything,

00:05:05.252 --> 00:05:07.210
you can walk up to the
cluster and you can say,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.590
I have this new great
object called a foo

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:11.339
and I want to start
managing a foo.

00:05:11.339 --> 00:05:13.130
I don't really want to
worry about storage.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:15.570
I don't want to really worry
about optimistic concurrency

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.116
and if multiple people update
something at the same time.

00:05:18.116 --> 00:05:19.990
I don't want to have to
do any of that logic.

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.110
But I have this new foo
object that I want to manage.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:26.797
And so you create a foo
resource on the server.

00:05:26.797 --> 00:05:28.630
And suddenly you have
this new resource type

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:31.171
that you can play around with
that works with all our command

00:05:31.171 --> 00:05:33.463
line tooling, that works with
all our client libraries.

00:05:33.463 --> 00:05:35.129
I'm pretty excited
about that, because I

00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:36.860
think that's going
to unlock people

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:38.720
building Kubernetes plugins.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.420
We're in a world
right now where if you

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.460
as a vendor, or you
as a third party,

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.030
or even inside of
Google, if you want

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.820
to deploy something
inside of Kubernetes,

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:48.510
you have to land it
in our code base.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:49.960
You have to get it committed.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:53.590
You have to go through
our code review process.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:56.300
You have to do
all of this stuff.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.620
And then we have to
maintain it, and so it's

00:05:58.620 --> 00:05:59.700
awkward on both sides.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.190
It's awkward for the
owner who really-- they

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:02.780
want to own that
thing independently.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:04.840
And it's awkward
for us, because we

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.650
have to-- when we
change something--

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.640
we've got to fix your code, even
though we might not actually

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.020
understand your code that well.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:10.850
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:12.330
BRENDAN BURNS: And so, I'm
really excited because I think

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:15.260
it's going to enable a lot more
rapid development and a lot

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.227
more distribution
of ownership that's

00:06:17.227 --> 00:06:19.060
going to make the project
even more scalable

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:19.967
than it already is.

00:06:19.967 --> 00:06:21.550
So, that's probably
the thing that I'm

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:23.170
most excited about for 1.3.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.080
Ubernetes is another thing
that I think is pretty awesome.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.371
There's going to be some talk
and discussion about that

00:06:27.371 --> 00:06:28.645
here as well, I think.

00:06:28.645 --> 00:06:30.020
And that's basically
enabling you

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:34.309
to manage multiple clusters,
taking a global view

00:06:34.309 --> 00:06:36.600
on your cluster footprint
instead of an individual data

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:36.940
center view.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:38.770
So, I'm some pretty
excited about that as well.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:39.990
MARK MANDEL: No, I've
definitely been speaking

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.660
to a lot of people who want to
have that cross region or cross

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.289
cloud or different data center
type management solutions,

00:06:46.289 --> 00:06:48.080
and they're pretty
excited about Ubernetes.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:48.170
BRENDAN BURNS: For sure.

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:50.170
Yeah, that's one of those
ones where I was like,

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:53.222
can you at least let us get
done with what we started?

00:06:53.222 --> 00:06:55.180
And we were like, here
it's this awesome thing,

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.263
and then everyone's, like,
but what if I have two?

00:06:57.263 --> 00:06:59.560
And we're like, we
still [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:01.564
Pretty soon once it's
done, you're ready,

00:07:01.564 --> 00:07:02.980
they want to go
to the next thing.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:06.180
So, it's great to see that
starting to happen as well.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.270
Yeah, and again,
that's a great example.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:09.879
Google's doing a
lot of work there.

00:07:09.879 --> 00:07:11.670
Quinton Hoole is doing
a lot of work there,

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.910
but Huawei-- the Chinese
telecom company--

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:15.670
is also heavily involved
in that process.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.310
So again, it's
really nice to see

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:18.775
that community coming together.

00:07:18.775 --> 00:07:19.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:23.180
So, we know you're here
to speak at GCP Next,

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.060
so what are you going
to be talking about?

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.904
BRENDAN BURNS: We're mostly
going to be talking about 1.2.

00:07:27.904 --> 00:07:29.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: 1.2 market?

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.006
BRENDAN BURNS: Yeah,
some people are Cube-Con,

00:07:31.006 --> 00:07:33.380
but I think Cube Con is a
little bit more-- first of all,

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:35.340
it was in Europe,
and so I actually

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.870
didn't get to travel
because family and all that.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.190
And I think it's more of
a Kubernetes audience, not

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.975
necessarily a full
general cloud audience.

00:07:43.975 --> 00:07:45.350
And so, I think
here we're really

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:48.699
going to try and highlight
Kubernetes for people

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:50.990
who haven't seen it before,
Container Engine for people

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:51.570
who haven't seen it before.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.653
Not too much on that,
because I think we've mostly

00:07:53.653 --> 00:07:55.250
gone over that hurdle.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.990
But then a lot about what
is the stuff that we've

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.886
introduced, how does
it make building

00:07:59.886 --> 00:08:01.010
distributed systems easier.

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:02.650
Because at the end of the day,
that's what we're really about.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.580
It's like, let's build
reliable distributed systems

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:06.890
in an easier way.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:10.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, that
sounds like a great talk.

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:12.510
Were you here on time
to follow the keynote?

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.200
BRENDAN BURNS: I followed the
keynote on Twitter on the plane

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:14.930
on the way down.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:15.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Ah, nice.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.480
BRENDAN BURNS: I didn't actually
get a chance to be here.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:18.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What was
your favorite announcement?

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.790
What did you get the
most excited about?

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:22.415
BRENDAN BURNS: Well,
I mean, I'm pretty

00:08:22.415 --> 00:08:26.040
excited to see that the dem-- to
plus plus the demo that I did.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.820
So, I did this demo at
Cube-Con in December.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:31.910
I did this one million QPS demo.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.789
So, for Kelsey and other
folks to take that and really

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:38.516
take it to the next level, I
thought it was really awesome.

00:08:38.516 --> 00:08:40.890
I mean, that's a little bit
self-centered, I guess, but--

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:41.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's OK.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:41.365
It was an awesome demo
to be honest, yes.

00:08:41.365 --> 00:08:43.870
BRENDAN BURNS: I thought
that was pretty awesome,

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:47.190
so I'm hoping that
generates some buzz.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:49.310
Not a demo that was on
the stage this morning,

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:51.180
but another announcement that
went out this morning that I

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.596
thought was pretty
awesome as well

00:08:52.596 --> 00:08:57.400
was TensorFlow, which is the
open source machine learning

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.490
algorithm that we--
no, I shouldn't say we,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:02.137
but the folks at
Google have released.

00:09:02.137 --> 00:09:04.470
They just put out instructions
