00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:50.510
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:51.926
Why don't we have
a chat with her?

00:10:51.926 --> 00:10:54.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Today,
we are joined by Rae Wang

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.890
to talk to us about IAM.

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:58.099
How are you doing today, Rae?

00:10:58.099 --> 00:10:59.181
RAE WANG: I'm doing great.

00:10:59.181 --> 00:10:59.784
Thank you.

00:10:59.784 --> 00:11:01.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So, why don't you

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.240
tell us a little bit
about yourself first?

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:05.190
RAE WANG: OK, hi, I'm Rae.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:09.670
I'm a product manager working
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:13.200
I look after the
IAM part of GCP.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.820
I'm based in the
Google Seattle office,

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.410
and I have been at
Google for three years.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:21.510
Before that, I worked in
other software companies

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:22.690
for over a decade.

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:24.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very
cool, and could you

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:28.930
tell us a little bit about
what is exactly IAM, you said?

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:31.700
RAE WANG: Yes, IAM,
it's three letters.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:36.030
It stands for Identity
and Access Management.

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:38.110
Well, if you're
security guru, you

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:40.410
might say it means
authentication

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:42.350
and authorization.

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:44.970
What it basically is
is there's two parts.

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:47.065
The I part and the AM part.

00:11:47.065 --> 00:11:50.640
The I part also is-- it
means authentication.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:55.640
It's how you define the
identity of a user or a program

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.882
and then be able to
verify the identity-- you

00:11:58.882 --> 00:12:00.340
are who you say
you are when you're

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:02.990
trying to access GCP resources.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:05.450
And then you have the AM part,
which is access management

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:08.850
and that's your access
policies, authorization.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:13.170
It's where you define who can
do what on which resources.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.160
So, then when you put
the two parts together,

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:18.160
they safeguard your
Google Cloud resources

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:21.070
and they make sure that only
people that you want them

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:23.250
to access certain
part of GCP can

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:25.190
access the specific
resources that they

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:26.500
are allowed to access.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.790
MARK MANDEL: So, you mentioned
resources a number of times.

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.040
What does resources mean?

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:32.567
Is that just parts
of the console

00:12:32.567 --> 00:12:35.150
or does that mean actual things
that are running in the cloud?

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:37.290
What does that refer to?

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:39.660
RAE WANG: Well, resource
at the most basic level

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.540
is a thing you can use in Cloud.

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:45.280
For example, a virtual
machine is a resource.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:48.350
A cloud storage
packet is a resource.

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:50.800
But then we also lifted a
little bit higher because just

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.730
managing the [INAUDIBLE]
a few thousand

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:55.340
VMs gets a little cumbersome.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.120
So, then we have
high level resources.

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:01.310
Things like a project
in GCP is a resource,

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:03.350
and it's basically a
resource container.

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:05.730
Underneath a project you
have your actual resources

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.480
like VMs, and storage,
and BigQuery, and so on.

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:11.220
But we also make the
project itself a resource so

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:15.530
you can search policies
on a project level,

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:18.000
and then they can be
inherited by all the smaller

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:19.670
resources underneath a project.

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:22.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so that
sounds a little bit like when

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:27.350
we said we had project editors,
project owners, and project

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:28.420
readers.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:31.030
What is the big
difference between that,

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.040
what we used to have, and IAM?

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.770
RAE WANG: Yeah, so, project
owner, and editor, and viewers,

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:41.690
the roles you had in the
past, they were IAM version 0.

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:44.850
They were the most
basic set of permissions

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:46.850
that you could grant to
users to basically share

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:48.370
your project with them.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.470
Now, the problem that
a lot of customers had

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:52.460
was that they're
not granular enough.

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:55.950
So, for example, if you want to
let your developers just look

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:59.220
at the logs in a project
so they can do debugging,

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.340
you have to grant
them a project viewer,

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:03.280
and by granting them
a pure project viewer

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:05.780
they can not only see the
logs but they can also

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:09.250
read all the content of the
data store and storage bucket.

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:11.330
And if you're running
a reapplication,

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:13.320
the data in these
storage systems

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:16.030
could be your sensitive
PII customer data,

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:18.250
so it's not really
great for developers

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:20.210
to have access to that.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.950
Another example we've
heard is a customer

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:26.210
uses a continuous integration
system to deploy updates

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:28.240
to their GAE app.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:32.610
And when they do that, they had
to give the CI system a project

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:35.110
editor role, but by
having that role,

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:36.780
the CI system could
also go in and again

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:39.680
modify other things in their
storage bucket, other things

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:41.300
in compute, in networking.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:43.120
That's not really
their intention,

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:47.130
so these roles-- we're
very first step, step zero,

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:49.140
but they were not granular
enough for anybody

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:51.030
to really secure
their resources.

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:53.330
And that's why we've
had to push out

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:56.780
the real IAM system
with granular control

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:58.040
in the last few weeks.

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:00.590
MARK MANDEL: Cool, so it
sounds like IAM is particularly

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:02.007
focused around security.

00:15:02.007 --> 00:15:04.340
Are there other reasons you
might want to use it outside

00:15:04.340 --> 00:15:06.476
of security, or is that
pretty much the big reason?

00:15:06.476 --> 00:15:08.350
RAE WANG: Well, security
is a big part of it.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:09.930
Certainly securing
your resources

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:12.150
is the main intention of IAM.

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:14.020
But then there's
also, for example,

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:19.640
if you are using automation
to access GCP, if you're

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:24.250
programmatically creating VMs
or manage your storage packets,

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:26.720
you need to let your
program access GCP

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:28.570
and then now you have
the problem of how

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:32.550
do I verify that this program
is the one I'm intending

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:33.790
to have it access GCP.

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:36.220
So, another part
of IAM what we do

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:39.560
is to give you a feature card
or service account, which

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:43.530
is a way for you to create
a rollout account so

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.662
that your programs
can call GCP API.

00:15:45.662 --> 00:15:46.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:49.140
So that basically
means that now I

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:52.680
could have some bots that
do something very specific

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.965
and I could say they can
only do exactly what they're

00:15:54.965 --> 00:15:55.590
supposed to do.

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:58.750
So, if for any reason someone
hacked it or whatever,

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:00.450
they couldn't do
anything extra, right?

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:01.510
RAE WANG: Yeah, exactly.

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:04.060
And then that's actually
where using service accounts

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.530
is where we see a
lot of times people

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:08.850
want very, very
granular permissions.

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:11.734
Your people might do a
broader job function.

00:16:11.734 --> 00:16:13.150
But the service
accounts sometimes

00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:17.570
can do very specific things
just to manage storage

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:19.830
or just to manage
a Pub/Sub topic,

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:21.400
and that's where
we see permissions

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:23.310
can get very granular.

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:26.650
MARK MANDEL: Excellent, so if
I want to get started with IAM,

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:31.580
how do I control what resources
I can access if I'm a VM,

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:32.840
or how do I do that?

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:34.210
Is it a command line interface?

00:16:34.210 --> 00:16:35.570
Can I do it through the website?

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:36.361
Is it programmatic?

00:16:36.361 --> 00:16:38.390
What tools have I
got available to me?

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:42.660
RAE WANG: So, we have support
for accessing your IAM policies

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:46.780
through both UI, API, and CLI.

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:50.789
And you set the IAM
policy on a resource.

00:16:50.789 --> 00:16:52.205
And we just talked
about resource.

00:16:52.205 --> 00:16:55.335
It could be an individual
thing like a Pub/Sub topic,

00:16:55.335 --> 00:16:56.890
or it can be a
project, or it can

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:59.200
be above a project
like an organization.

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:00.970
So, for every
resource level that

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:04.569
supports resource-specific
IAM policies,

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:08.030
there is a common meta
API [? get to set ?]

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:11.030
policy and test
permissions, as well as

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:14.294
a common UI widget to
view the permissions

00:17:14.294 --> 00:17:15.710
and change any of
the permissions.

00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:18.510
So, it will look very
similar, but it can

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:22.012
be done on different resources.

00:17:22.012 --> 00:17:23.720
MARK MANDEL: So, the
other thought I just

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:25.160
had was around resources.

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:27.510
So, say I have a thousand VMs.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:29.510
Am I able to group those
together in some way?

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:32.010
I don't want to obviously be
setting permissions on each one

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:32.630
individually.

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:34.030
I mean, maybe I could do
that programmatically,

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:35.155
but is there an easier way?

00:17:35.155 --> 00:17:37.520
RAE WANG: Yeah, yeah,
that's a very good question.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:41.220
So, we talked about how
we layer the resources.

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:44.880
You have your basic VMs and
above that is a project,

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:48.310
and that's often where you
group the resources that

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:49.720
belong to a single service.

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.620
For example, that's a
basic level of grouping

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:56.330
and a project is there
for you to set permissions

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:57.870
so they get inherited to VMs.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.230
It's a mechanism
to group our VMs.

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:03.540
But it's also the
basic unit for you

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.570
to set down things like
billing, and quota,

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:08.890
and a lot of the other
control mechanisms.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.250
And that bar project,
some of our customers--

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:13.580
Google itself
included-- as you get

00:18:13.580 --> 00:18:16.320
into [? call ?] usage over time,
you end up with many projects,

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:19.910
because you moved many
services onto cloud.

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:23.390
Google itself, we've got more
than 100,000 projects, and then

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:25.120
of course managing
them individually

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:27.340
is also going to be cumbersome.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:30.850
So, we have introduced this
higher level resource called

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:33.270
the organization
node, and that's

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:35.150
the root node for your company.

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:38.165
And then all the projects
that employees in your company

00:18:38.165 --> 00:18:40.990
have created would belong
to your organization node.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:44.540
So, you can now manage
resources on the org level,

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.940
on the project level,
and then in some cases

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:49.560
for certain resources, on the
individual resource level.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:52.750
All of these levels,
policies inherited down

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:54.300
this resource hierarchy.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.910
So, for example, if you
grant your networking team

00:18:56.910 --> 00:18:59.960
in your company, the network
admin role on the organization

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:03.180
level, that means that they have
the network admin permission

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:05.170
on all of the projects
and all of the networks

00:19:05.170 --> 00:19:07.580
in these projects
underneath the organization,

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:09.889
so you don't have to
permission them individually.

00:19:09.889 --> 00:19:11.930
MARK MANDEL: Cool, so I
guess that means-- great,

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:15.490
so that means you can really
choose which level it is

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:17.150
you want to be able to pick at.

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:18.920
You can go all the
way at the top, which

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:22.610
is that org hierarchy to,
say, all way down to VMs,

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:24.579
and just levels in
between as well.

00:19:24.579 --> 00:19:25.870
RAE WANG: Yeah, that's correct.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:30.140
So, on the org
level it's typically

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:33.940
your company central network
admin or essential admins,

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:36.030
people who are
looking after having

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:38.600
oversight over the entire
company's resources.

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:40.130
And on project
level, it typically

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:43.650
is about your team, who are
the people who are taking

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:45.070
care of a service together.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.290
And then, on the
individual resource level,

00:19:47.290 --> 00:19:51.630
right now we have the
support for a few pops up

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:54.510
resources and then GCS
BigQuery, but we can

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:56.440
add those two things like VMs.

00:19:56.440 --> 00:20:00.090
So, that's where if you
share the amount of queries

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:02.330
[INAUDIBLE] level often
when you, for example, need

00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:05.730
to let a particular developer
debug a particular VM,

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:09.334
or a particular storage
bucket for a little bit,

00:20:09.334 --> 00:20:10.750
or you're sharing
a storage bucket

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:13.790
through an application when
you get very, very specific

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:15.880
about a particular resource.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:16.630
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:19.260
So I have to ask
the question, I know

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:21.900
our users were clamoring
for IAM for quite a while

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:23.920
and it took us a while
to get out the door.

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:27.771
But we talked about this a
bit before this conversation,

00:20:27.771 --> 00:20:30.270
and I think you had a really
good answer for that situation.

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:31.395
Would you like to share it?

00:20:31.395 --> 00:20:33.930
RAE WANG: I think the question
is why did it take so long?

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:35.460
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, pretty much.

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:37.510
RAE WANG: Well,
a system like IAM

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:40.740
is something that almost
pretty much every single one

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:42.550
of our APIs go through.

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:47.340
Every time you make an API call
to GCP, it does an IAM check.

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:50.560
So, when we were
replacing the old system

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:53.000
to get it to the new platform
so we can implement more

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.360
granular control, we
really want to make sure

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:58.130
that it doesn't hurt
reliability anyway.

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:01.100
We need to make sure that
from behavior point of view,

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:04.280
from reliability, and latency,
and every perspective,

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:07.180
the new system is at par or
better than the old system

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:08.245
before we replace it.

00:21:08.245 --> 00:21:09.870
So, we had to be very
careful about it.

00:21:09.870 --> 00:21:13.050
It's like replacing
your plane's core engine

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:15.730
while flying it while
making sure your plane

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:18.320
is still stable and
doesn't drop in height.

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:21.040
So, we've had to do
extensive testing

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:23.040
and putting a lot of
infrastructure to make sure

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:24.920
that that switch is very smooth.

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:28.190
What we ended up doing was we
had the old system running.

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:29.640
We had a new system up.

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:32.370
And then while all
the real traffic

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:34.260
was going through the
old system, we sending

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:37.500
parallel the same traffic
through the new system,

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:40.000
play the Doc launch,
and then through Logs,

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:42.380
when we saw that all the
behaviors are identical

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:44.760
and the reliability
and latency are better,

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:47.860
only then did we switch
over to the new system.

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:49.780
And even after we
switched, we're

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:53.570
still keeping the old system
up for a while as a backup.

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:56.589
We want to make sure that this
transition is really smooth,

00:21:56.589 --> 00:21:58.380
it doesn't hurt any of
the user experience,

00:21:58.380 --> 00:21:59.940
and it's only for the better.

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:00.856
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:22:00.856 --> 00:22:05.260
So, I have a question, and
maybe the answer is no and then

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:08.750
it's a future request.

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:11.510
So, I'm imagining a
world where you actually

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:15.555
have just one project and a
bunch of computation instances.

00:22:15.555 --> 00:22:18.520
And what you want to say
is that someone has access

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:21.690
to those instances as
long as the instance has

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:23.750
a specific tag.

00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:25.870
Is that something
that we provide?

00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:27.260
RAE WANG: Not today.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:30.860
Right now you can-- when you
grant somebody an instance

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:34.030
admin role on a project
they get access to all

00:22:34.030 --> 00:22:37.600
the instances in that project.

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:39.330
You could do a
little subdividing.

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:43.380
One thing we have shipped
is per VM service accounts,

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:48.210
so you could, by granting people
access on a per service account

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:51.820
basis, you could
say this person is

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:55.970
allowed to [? speed up VM ?]
but only of a certain identity.

00:22:55.970 --> 00:22:59.180
But we don't yet have
the-- you can have access

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:02.270
to everything in this project
only when it's labeled x.

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:04.690
And I have heard from
a number of customers

00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:06.100
the desire to do that.

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:09.945
We're definitely considering
that on our road map.

00:23:09.945 --> 00:23:11.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:14.290
So since you're already
talking about the road map,

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:16.690
what else can we expect
to see in the future?

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:18.810
RAE WANG: So, one of the
first things we often

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:20.494
get as soon as we
tell people look here

00:23:20.494 --> 00:23:22.660
at the 20-something beautiful
and new granular roles

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:26.000
we have shipped, but what about
this other role I really want?

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:29.260
BigQuery comes up,
monitoring comes up.

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:30.680
A fair number of
them-- it depends

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:32.880
on the particular products
you're using-- you

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:37.560
want roles for the products you
care about so you can give out

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:39.130
very granular permissions.

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:41.990
So, we're definitely
working on shipping

00:23:41.990 --> 00:23:46.460
the next batch of roles
based on different services.

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:49.260
And we're also working
on giving you the ability

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:53.110
to make custom roles,
because for-- we're

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:55.860
hoping that in the
80% case you can just

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.600
make use of the
out of box roles,

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.260
and that's the easiest path.

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:00.960
It saves you a lot of work.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:04.400
But for some of our more
advanced customers-- especially

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:06.450
in the case of, for
example, service accounts--

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:09.720
they want to tailor to very
specific permission sets.

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:12.270
So, we're also working on
making it possible to create

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:14.180
your own custom roles.

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:17.860
And then beyond that, also
we talked about the ability

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:20.490
to let you specify a
permission based on labels.

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:22.950
So, we call these conditions.

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:25.510
This set of syntax
allows you to say, yes,

00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:27.720
grant this person such and
such role and such result,

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:30.550
but also only if either
the person or resource meet

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:32.080
such set of conditions.

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:33.970
And these could be labels.

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:37.650
It could be time-based.

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:38.810
It could be IP-based.

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:40.840
So, there are a number
of additional policies

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:44.560
that we're looking at that could
be added to the IAM syntax.

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:46.400
And also, of course,
once we allow

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:49.472
you to use all these granular
roles within a very custom way,

00:24:49.472 --> 00:24:51.180
the next problem you're
going to run into

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:53.830
is that they become hard to
manage and hard to debug,

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:55.470
so we definitely
also want to invest

00:24:55.470 --> 00:25:00.860
in the debugging experience to
make the policy language not

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:02.485
only rich but also easy to use.

00:25:02.485 --> 00:25:04.860
MARK MANDEL: So, that sounds
like some really interesting

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:05.522
features.

00:25:05.522 --> 00:25:07.480
I just wanted to backtrack
for just one second,

00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:09.390
because we've mentioned
it a few times.

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:10.800
We talked about
service accounts,

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:13.091
but I don't think we really
talked about what those are

00:25:13.091 --> 00:25:15.140
and what the relationships
those have with IAM.

00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:18.050
Can you explain what they are
and how that ties directly

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:18.930
into IAM?

00:25:18.930 --> 00:25:20.641
RAE WANG: That's a
really good question.

00:25:20.641 --> 00:25:22.890
So, typically when you think
about-- when people think

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:24.970
about identities they
think about users,

00:25:24.970 --> 00:25:27.130
a person who can type
a username password

00:25:27.130 --> 00:25:28.830
or press a hardware key.

00:25:28.830 --> 00:25:31.220
But sometimes,
especially when you're

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:34.220
interacting with clouds for API,
there's a lot of automation,

00:25:34.220 --> 00:25:37.090
a lot of applications people
build that talk to APIs

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:39.660
not necessarily from
a user as a person,

00:25:39.660 --> 00:25:41.210
but rather as a program.

00:25:41.210 --> 00:25:43.530
And a program can't go and
type in a username password.

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:45.880
They don't work the same
way as human beings,

00:25:45.880 --> 00:25:50.000
so we need to give these
robots an identity as well.

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:53.460
And that's where service
accounts came to be.

00:25:53.460 --> 00:25:58.002
We need to have a way to manage
identities for automations

00:25:58.002 --> 00:25:59.710
either around App
Engine, Compute Engine,

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:01.170
or around somewhere
else and have

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:04.515
them authenticate and be able
to access APIs in a secure way

00:26:04.515 --> 00:26:05.720
as well.

00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:07.402
So, that's a service account.

00:26:07.402 --> 00:26:09.360
Now, since we're talking
about service accounts

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:13.820
account-type username passwords,
they're authenticated by a key.

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:17.180
So one or more keys can be
created for each service

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:18.690
account on GCP.

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:20.800
And then you can
basically use the key

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:23.020
in your program to let your
program authenticate who

00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:26.462
has the key to prove who they
are to access the resources.

00:26:26.462 --> 00:26:28.920
Well, if your program happens
to run on App Engine, Compute

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.480
Engine, then your life
becomes a lot easier,

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:33.809
because we actually
manage the keys for you.

00:26:33.809 --> 00:26:35.350
The keys are actually
sensitive data,

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:37.780
because once you have them,
you can prove who you are

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:40.180
and access resources.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:44.110
If you're running on one of
the GCP compute platforms,

00:26:44.110 --> 00:26:47.470
then we keep the keys for you.

00:26:47.470 --> 00:26:49.110
We [INAUDIBLE] rotate
the keys for you

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:52.220
so you don't have to worry about
losing them or leaking them.

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:54.460
And if you're running a
program somewhere else,

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:56.870
you can download the keys to
be used in your own program,

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:59.670
but please take care of
them because they basically

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:01.212
open a door to your resources.

00:27:01.212 --> 00:27:02.170
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:04.586
Well, I think we're running
out of time just a little bit,

00:27:04.586 --> 00:27:07.190
but is there anything
about IAM that you

00:27:07.190 --> 00:27:09.280
want to mention that maybe
we haven't covered yet

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:11.071
or haven't talked about yet?

00:27:11.071 --> 00:27:12.820
RAE WANG: So, one thing
I want to bring up

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:16.170
is in my conversation with a
lot of customers, what we often

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:20.800
find is that people tend to
think of IAM when they have

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:25.960
a security incident, when things
get a little out of control.

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:28.320
But the best way to
actually plan for IAM

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:31.860
is to plan ahead before you
get to the point where you go,

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:34.362
holy crap, I've got to
tighten down security.

00:27:34.362 --> 00:27:36.820
A lot of the planning is--
we'll talk about on the resource

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:40.000
level how you set up your
organization, how you lay out

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.880
your projects, and
what groups you set up.

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:45.130
And some of these
higher level concepts

00:27:45.130 --> 00:27:48.130
are much easier to think about,
to decide, and to lay out

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:50.730
before you've already
got an application

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:53.690
running, last minute trying
to tighten up security.

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:56.545
So, do a lot of
thinking ahead of time

00:27:56.545 --> 00:27:58.420
about how you want to
arrange your resources,

00:27:58.420 --> 00:27:59.836
how you want to
set your policies,

00:27:59.836 --> 00:28:01.816
and that will help your
apps to go a long way.

00:28:01.816 --> 00:28:02.732
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:28:02.732 --> 00:28:04.830
That sounds like a very
good piece of advice.

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:06.820
Well, thank you very
much for your time, Rae.

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:07.980
I learned a lot.

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:08.850
RAE WANG: Thank you.

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:10.730
I really enjoyed talking
to you about IAM.

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:12.130
MARK MANDEL: Much
appreciated, thanks so much.

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:12.800
RAE WANG: Thank you.

00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:14.800
MARK MANDEL: Thanks so much
to Rae for joining us today--

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:16.370
really appreciate
having that chat.

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:19.620
I have a much better
appreciation now of Identity

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:21.160
and Access Management.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:22.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:28:22.540 --> 00:28:23.550
Yeah, very interesting.

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:25.140
I learned a lot,
and I'm actually

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:28.840
going to start using that next
time I have a bigger project.

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:30.602
MARK MANDEL:
Definitely, absolutely.

00:28:30.602 --> 00:28:32.810
But why don't we talk about
the question of the week?

00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:35.550
I think this one's a
little interesting one.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:38.230
So, we've had cloud
storage for a while.

00:28:38.230 --> 00:28:40.220
So, if you have a
static binary asset

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:42.640
you want to put in the
cloud and have edge caching

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:44.350
all over the world,
cloud storage

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:46.470
has always been great for that.

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:47.970
But the question
always comes up,

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:50.820
like, what if I'm
dynamically creating content,

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:53.590
maybe images, or sound
files, or movies,

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:56.620
and I want to also have that
edge caching around the world.

00:28:56.620 --> 00:28:57.550
How can I do that?

00:28:57.550 --> 00:29:01.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, even
if those images are just

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:03.220
stored in some hard
disk and you just

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:04.820
have a web server serving them?

00:29:04.820 --> 00:29:05.320
How do you--

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:06.028
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:29:06.028 --> 00:29:09.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So there's
actually a great thing

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:15.090
that we added recently, which is
what we call Google Cloud CDN.

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:18.242
And CDN stands for Content
Distribution Network.

00:29:18.242 --> 00:29:18.950
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:21.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And it's in beta.

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:23.680
And what this is is
basically just something

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:28.560
that you can add on top of any
HTTP or HTTPS load balancer

00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:33.650
and it allows you to do
exactly that, cache requests.

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:37.418
The way it works is just by the
simple-- the traditional HTTP,

00:29:37.418 --> 00:29:40.360
HTTPS caching mechanism.

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:43.120
So, you have those
headers and we're

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:45.590
going to cache the responses
to the requests that

00:29:45.590 --> 00:29:49.400
have the method is get and
the answer is like 200.

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:53.960
And there's a bunch
of specific constrains

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:56.210
to decide if something
is cached or not,

00:29:56.210 --> 00:29:58.700
so just go check it out on
the [? documentation. ?]

00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:02.970
The interesting thing is that
it is very, very easy to set up.

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:04.520
So, once you have
your load balancer,

00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:07.510
everything you need to do
is just click on a check box

00:30:07.510 --> 00:30:08.400
and that it.

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:13.340
You're ready to go, and
now you have a cache.

00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:16.730
As soon as you have cache,
the next question is cool,

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:18.990
how do I invalidate it?

00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:20.840
That always happens.

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:22.150
And it's actually very simple.

00:30:22.150 --> 00:30:24.300
If you've used Memcache
on App Engine where

00:30:24.300 --> 00:30:26.560
you have that little console
where you can go and look

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:29.880
for a key and just remove
it, same thing pretty much

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:31.280
but instead of
going to Memcache,

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:33.940
you're going to go to the load
balancing page in networking

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:35.770
and find the
caching tab and just

00:30:35.770 --> 00:30:37.210
